research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-120-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-121-**Given:** A natural $m \ge 1$, reals $a < b$, and a function $f : [a,b] \to \mathbb{R}^{m}$ with the hypotheses of the clause under discussion; points $s, t \in [a,b]$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-122-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md:123:[L1] The vector-valued derivative and integral are componentwise: $f'(c)_i = f_i'(c)$, and $f$ is integrable exactly when every $f_i$ is, with $\bigl(\int_a^b f\bigr)_i = \int_a^b f_i$; equality of two elements of $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ is equality of all their coordinates ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-124-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-125-[L2] The second fundamental theorem: if $G$ is differentiable on $[a,b]$ with $G' = g$ and $g$ is integrable on $[a,b]$, then $\int_a^b g = G(b)-G(a)$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[def-derivative]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-126-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-127-[L3] The mean value inequality on a subinterval ([[thm-mean-value-inequality]]): for $s<t$, $f$ continuous on $[s,t]$ and differentiable on $(s,t)$ with $\lVert f'\rVert_2 \le M$ there, $\lVert f(t)-f(s)\rVert_2 \le M(t-s)$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-128-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-129-[L4] Restricting the domain of a function preserves a limit and its value, the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ condition then quantifying over fewer points; in particular if $f$ is differentiable at $c$ as a function on $[a,b]$ and $c$ is a limit point of $[s,t] \subseteq [a,b]$, then the restriction of $f$ to $[s,t]$ is differentiable at $c$ with the same derivative ([[def-function-limit]], [[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-interval]]).
