research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-845-## Proof
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-846-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-847-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-848-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-849-1.1 Let $x, y \in I$. If $x = y$ then $|f(x)-f(y)| = |0| = 0$ and $M|x-y| = M \cdot 0 = 0$, so the asserted inequality holds. Assume therefore $x \ne y$, and put $u := \min\{x,y\}$ and $v := \max\{x,y\}$, so that $u, v \in I$, $u < v$, and $|x - y| = v - u = |v - u|$ by [L5]. [given, L5]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-850-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md:851:2.1 By [L2] the segment $[u,v]$ lies in $I$ and is nondegenerate; the restriction $f|_{[u,v]}$ is continuous on $[u,v]$ by [L4]; and each $x' \in (u,v)$ is interior to $I$ by [L2], hence a point at which $f$ is differentiable with $|f'(x')| \le M$, while $x'$ is a limit point of $[u,v]$ by [L3], so $f|_{[u,v]}$ is differentiable at $x'$ with the same derivative. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L4]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-852-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-853-3.1 By step 2.1 the function $f|_{[u,v]}$ satisfies the hypotheses of [L1], so fix $c \in (u,v)$ with $f(v) - f(u) = f'(c)\,(v-u)$. [step 2.1, L1, choose]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-854-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-855-4.1 Taking absolute values in step 3.1 and using $|uw| = |u||w|$ gives $|f(v)-f(u)| = |f'(c)|\,|v-u|$. The point $c$ lies in $(u,v)$, hence is interior to $I$ by step 2.1, so $0 \le |f'(c)| \le M$; and $0 \le |v-u| \le |v-u|$. So [L8] gives $|f'(c)|\,|v-u| \le M\,|v-u|$, whence $|f(v)-f(u)| \le M\,|v-u|$. Since $\{u,v\} = \{x,y\}$ and $|f(v)-f(u)| = |f(x)-f(y)|$ by [L5], and $|v-u| = |x-y|$ by step 1.1, this is $|f(x)-f(y)| \le M\,|x-y|$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, L5, L8]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-856-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-857-5.1 The pair $x, y \in I$ was arbitrary and the case $x = y$ was settled in step 1.1, so $|f(x)-f(y)| \le M|x-y|$ for all $x, y \in I$. By [L6] that is the statement that $f$ is Lipschitz with constant $M$ on $I$, and by [L7] such an $f$ is uniformly continuous on $I$. [step 1.1, step 4.1, L6, L7] ∎
