research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-944-[L4] First fundamental theorem: if $u$ is integrable on $[p,q]$ with $p<q$ and continuous at $c \in [p,q]$, then $x \mapsto \int_p^x u$ has derivative $u(c)$ at $c$ as a function on $[p,q]$; written out, for every real $\varepsilon>0$ there is a real $\delta>0$ with $\bigl|\bigl(\int_p^x u - \int_p^c u\bigr)/(x-c) - u(c)\bigr| < \varepsilon$ for every $x \in [p,q]$ with $0<|x-c|<\delta$ ([[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[def-derivative]], [[def-function-limit]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-945-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-946-[L5] Second fundamental theorem: if $H$ is differentiable at every point of $[p,q]$ with $H'$ integrable there, then $\int_p^q H' = H(q)-H(p)$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-947-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-948-[L6] If $u$ is continuous on an order-convex $I$ and differentiable with $u' = 0$ at every interior point of $I$, then $u$ is constant on $I$ ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-949-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md:950:[L7] A differentiable function is continuous, and the restriction of a function differentiable at $c$ to a subset still having $c$ as a limit point is differentiable at $c$ with the same derivative; every point of a nondegenerate interval is a limit point of it ([[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]], [[def-derivative]], [[def-interval]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-951-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r6-cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound.prompt.md-952-[L8] Ordered-field arithmetic and minima of two reals: the order is total and transitive, and $\min\{s,t\}$ is a real that is $\le$ both ([[def-max-min]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
